# Append-only audit logs union-merge instead of conflicting (#128).
#
# ## The failure this prevents
#
# `.operum/audit/*.jsonl` files are APPEND-ONLY logs written automatically by
# agent tooling — `protected-file-events.jsonl` gets a row every time a branch
# restores the auto-generated `repo-map.toml`, which is to say on essentially
# every branch that runs a build. Two concurrent branches therefore append to
# the same file at the same EOF, constantly.
#
# Without a merge driver that conflict presents as an ordinary "pick a side":
#
#     <<<<<<< HEAD
#     {"ts":"…","branch":"my-branch",…}
#     =======
#     {"ts":"…","branch":"other",…}   × 4
#     >>>>>>> main
#
# Both one-key resolutions silently destroy history — `--ours` drops the four
# rows from main, `--theirs` drops the one from this branch. Neither leaves a
# trace, and **no test can catch it after the fact**: an append-only log has no
# append-only assertion, so a dropped row is simply a row that was never there.
#
# That is not hypothetical. It fired twice in one session (PRs #164 and #165),
# and it is the same shape as the incident the concealment doctrine records,
# where a whole-file restore destroyed three entries.
#
# `merge=union` is a built-in git driver: it keeps BOTH sides' lines. For a
# line-oriented JSONL log that is not a heuristic, it is the correct answer —
# the union of two sets of appended records IS the merged log.
#
# ## Two patterns, because `*` does not cross a `/`
#
# gitattributes patterns follow gitignore syntax, where `*` matches within one
# path segment only. So the first line below covers the logs sitting directly in
# `.operum/audit/`, and the second covers the one-file-per-entry captures under
# `.operum/audit/concealment-reminders/`.
#
# Those per-entry files are named to avoid collisions and so should never
# conflict — but the directory already contains names disambiguated by hand with
# `-a` / `-b` suffixes, which is evidence that same-name collisions do happen.
# When one does, union is still the right merge for a JSONL file, so the second
# line costs nothing and closes the gap rather than assuming it away.
.operum/audit/*.jsonl merge=union
.operum/audit/**/*.jsonl merge=union

# ## Deliberately NOT here: `* text=auto`
#
# A fresh `.gitattributes` invites adding line-ending normalisation, and this
# one does not. Enabling it would renormalise every file in the repository on
# the next checkout — a large, unrelated diff riding along with a two-line fix,
# on a change whose whole purpose is to stop content going missing. If the
# project wants it, it deserves its own PR and its own verification.
#
# ## Scope note for anyone relying on this
#
# `merge=union` is a driver applied by whichever client performs the merge, so
# it only takes effect once this file is checked out on that side. It does not
# retroactively fix branches already in flight — those still need a manual
# union — and it does not protect against a hand-edit or a whole-file restore,
# which is what `.github/scripts/check-audit-append-only.mjs` is for.
